                                ;============================================================
                                ; SEARCH  (/ ? n N)
                                ;
                                ; vi's search on WordMaster's matcher.  What it is NOT: vim searches with a
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-100


                                ; REGULAR EXPRESSION and this searches for the literal string typed.  An 8080
                                ; has no room for a regex engine; COMMANDS.md records the deviation.  (WM's own
                                ; search wildcards -- ^A any, ^S separator, ^O not -- still reach WCMATCH from
                                ; here, because MATCHF/MATCHB compare through it, but they are WordMaster's
                                ; syntax, not vim's, and nothing advertises them.)
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SRCH, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - '/' starts one character PAST the cursor and lands ON the match's first
                                ;     character; '?' finds the last match that ends before the cursor.
                                ;   - 'wrapscan' is on: a search that runs off one end of the file starts
                                ;     again at the other, so '/' on the file's ONLY match finds it again.
                                ;   - 'n' repeats the last search's DIRECTION and 'N' reverses it, so 'n'
                                ;     after a '?' keeps going backward.
                                ;   - An empty pattern ('/' then <CR>) repeats the last pattern.
                                ;   - A count repeats the search: '2/a' is the second match, '15n' fifteen
                                ;     n's.  Not found leaves the cursor exactly where it was, at any count.
                                ;   - The window is placed as 'G' places it -- least scroll when the match is
                                ;     near, centred when it is far -- which is why this ends in GPLACE.
                                ;
                                ; This replaces a byte-at-a-time search that ran through CHARAT over logical
                                ; offsets: it could only see the resident window (so a match further into a
                                ; paged file did not exist), had no '?' or 'N', and failed silently.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; SUBSTITUTE  (:s  :%s  :N,Ms  and the 'g' flag)
                                ;
                                ; vi's substitute on WordMaster's matcher, so it finds the same LITERAL string
                                ; the search does -- not a regular expression.  COMMANDS.md records that
                                ; deviation for '/' and it is the same one here, which is why nothing below
                                ; honours '&', '\1' or '~'.  The pattern goes into the SEARCH's own buffer
                                ; because vim's ':s' also sets the last search pattern, so 'n' afterwards
                                ; repeats it.
                                ;
                                ; Recorded from vim before any of this was written (VIM_SUBST, accept_vi.py):
                                ;   - ':s' changes the FIRST match on the cursor's line, ':s...g' every match,
                                ;     and the scan goes on PAST the replacement, so a replacement that
                                ;     contains the pattern is not substituted again.
                                ;   - the cursor lands on the first non-blank of the LAST line changed, placed
                                ;     exactly as 'G' places a line -- which is what GZONE before and GPLACE
                                ;     after are for, and it is measured: ':12800,12800s' leaves the last line
                                ;     on row 22 (top 012778) and ':100,105s' leaves line 105 on row 11.
                                ;   - a pattern that is nowhere in the range changes nothing and moves nothing.
                                ;
                                ; A whole substitute is ONE change for 'u': the second UNDBEG, at the first
                                ; match, is what makes the undo's cursor the first match (where vim leaves it)
                                ; rather than the line the ':' was typed on.  A substitute of more than about a
                                ; dozen lines overruns the undo region, which is accepted -- but it SAYS so,
                                ; both when it happens and when 'u' is then pressed.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;EXADDR -- the optional line range before an ex command: '%' (the whole file),
                                ;'N', or 'N,M'.  SRNGF = 0 when there is none, and SRNG2 = 0FFFFH is the last
                                ;line, whatever it turns out to be.  HL comes back on the command letter.
                                ;
                                ; It does NOT consume the blanks after itself: VC_EX's SKIPB does that, so
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-101


                                ; ':2,4 s/a/Z/' reads as ':2,4s/a/Z/' the way ex has always taken it.  The one
                                ; place a blank is still refused is INSIDE the range, around the comma
                                ; (':2, 4s'), which ex accepts and this does not -- the comma is read here off
                                ; DE mid-number and skipping there costs more than the position is worth.
  206F'                         EXADDR:
  206F'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2070'   32 26D0'              	STA	SRNGF
  2073'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2074'   FE 25                 	CPI	'%'
  2076'   CA 209F'              	JZ	EXA_AL
  2079'   EB                    	XCHG			; DE walks the line, HL takes the number
  207A'   CD 20B4'              	CALL	EXNUM
  207D'   DA 209D'              	JC	EXA_NO
  2080'   22 26D1'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  2083'   22 26D3'              	SHLD	SRNG2		; 'N' alone is the single line 'N,N'
  2086'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  2087'   FE 2C                 	CPI	','
  2089'   C2 2096'              	JNZ	EXA_YS
  208C'   13                    	INX	D
  208D'   CD 20B4'              	CALL	EXNUM
  2090'   DA 209D'              	JC	EXA_NO		; ',' with no number: not a range at all
  2093'   22 26D3'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  2096'                         EXA_YS:
  2096'   EB                    	XCHG
  2097'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2099'   32 26D0'              	STA	SRNGF
  209C'   C9                    	RET
  209D'                         EXA_NO:
  209D'   EB                    	XCHG
  209E'   C9                    	RET
  209F'                         EXA_AL:
  209F'   23                    	INX	H
  20A0'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  20A1'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  20A4'   22 26D1'              	SHLD	SRNG1
  20A7'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  20AA'   22 26D3'              	SHLD	SRNG2
  20AD'   E1                    	POP	H
  20AE'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  20B0'   32 26D0'              	STA	SRNGF
  20B3'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EXNUM -- a decimal number at DE -> HL, DE left past it.  CY when there is no
                                ;digit there at all.
  20B4'                         EXNUM:
  20B4'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  20B5'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  20B7'   D8                    	RC
  20B8'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  20BA'   3F                    	CMC
  20BB'   D8                    	RC
  20BC'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  20BF'                         EXN_LP:
  20BF'   1A                    	LDAX	D
  20C0'   D6 30                 	SUI	'0'
  20C2'   DA 20DC'              	JC	EXN_DN
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-102


  20C5'   FE 0A                 	CPI	10
  20C7'   D2 20DC'              	JNC	EXN_DN
  20CA'   13                    	INX	D
  20CB'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  20CC'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  20CD'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; BC = the digit
  20CF'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  20D0'   54                    	MOV	D,H
  20D1'   5D                    	MOV	E,L
  20D2'   29                    	DAD	H		; *2
  20D3'   29                    	DAD	H		; *4
  20D4'   19                    	DAD	D		; *5
  20D5'   29                    	DAD	H		; *10
  20D6'   D1                    	POP	D
  20D7'   09                    	DAD	B
  20D8'   C1                    	POP	B
  20D9'   C3 20BF'              	JMP	EXN_LP
  20DC'                         EXN_DN:
  20DC'   B7                    	ORA	A		; CY clear: there was a number
  20DD'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;EX_S -- ':s': the delimiter, the pattern, the replacement, an optional 'g'.
  20DE'                         EX_S:
  20DE'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  20DF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  20E0'   CA 2122'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s' alone -- repeating the last one is not
                                				;   built (COMMANDS.md)
  20E3'   32 26D5'              	STA	SDELIM
  20E6'   23                    	INX	H
  20E7'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  20EA'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; the pattern's capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  20ED'   4F                    	MOV	C,A
  20EE'   CD 2128'              	CALL	SCOPY
  20F1'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  20F2'   B7                    	ORA	A
  20F3'   CA 2122'              	JZ	SUBNOP		; ':s//x/' -- an empty pattern matches nothing
  20F6'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		; WM's RDSTR layout: the length below the string
  20F9'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  20FB'   32 26A1'              	STA	SHAVE		; 'n' repeats it, as after a '/'
  20FE'   11 26E2'              	LXI	D,SREPL
  2101'   0E 24                 	MVI	C,SREPMX
  2103'   CD 2128'              	CALL	SCOPY
  2106'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2107'   32 26E1'              	STA	SREPLN
  210A'   AF                    	XRA	A
  210B'   32 26D6'              	STA	SGFLAG
  210E'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  210F'   FE 67                 	CPI	'g'
  2111'   C2 211A'              	JNZ	EX_SG
  2114'   23                    	INX	H
  2115'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2117'   32 26D6'              	STA	SGFLAG
  211A'                         EX_SG:
  211A'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  211B'   B7                    	ORA	A
  211C'   C2 1F03'              	JNZ	EX_TRL		; 'Trailing chars: ' -- vim's, shortened
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-103


  211F'   C3 2148'              	JMP	SUBGO
  2122'                         SUBNOP:
  2122'   21 1FAF'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  2125'   C3 1F11'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;SCOPY -- the text at HL up to SDELIM (or the line's end) into [DE], C its
                                ;capacity: B = how many were taken, HL past the delimiter, and [DE] is left
                                ;0-terminated as WM's RDSTR leaves a string.
  2128'                         SCOPY:
  2128'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  212A'                         SCP_LP:
  212A'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  212B'   B9                    	CMP	C
  212C'   D2 2145'              	JNC	SCP_DN		; full: what is left stays on the line, and so
                                				;   comes back as trailing characters
  212F'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2130'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2131'   CA 2145'              	JZ	SCP_DN		; the line ended: no closing delimiter wanted
  2134'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2135'   21 26D5'              	LXI	H,SDELIM
  2138'   BE                    	CMP	M
  2139'   E1                    	POP	H
  213A'   CA 2144'              	JZ	SCP_SK
  213D'   12                    	STAX	D
  213E'   13                    	INX	D
  213F'   23                    	INX	H
  2140'   04                    	INR	B
  2141'   C3 212A'              	JMP	SCP_LP
  2144'                         SCP_SK:
  2144'   23                    	INX	H
  2145'                         SCP_DN:
  2145'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2146'   12                    	STAX	D
  2147'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBGO -- run the substitute.  GZONE comes first, before anything moves: the
                                ;landing is placed as 'G' places a line, and GPLACE measures it against the
                                ;screen's top line as it was when the command was typed.
  2148'                         SUBGO:
  2148'   CD 0EBF'              	CALL	GZONE
  214B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  214E'   22 26DF'              	SHLD	SLDLTS		; the walk moves the cursor; a substitute that
  2151'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   finds nothing puts both back, as the
  2154'   22 26DD'              	SHLD	SOFFS		;   search's SN_BAD does
  2157'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2158'   32 26D7'              	STA	SANY
  215B'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  215E'   22 26D9'              	SHLD	SBACK
  2161'   3A 26D0'              	LDA	SRNGF
  2164'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2165'   CA 218E'              	JZ	SUB_1L
                                	;--- ':N,Ms' / ':%s': to the range's first line, then walk it ---
  2168'   2A 26D1'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  216B'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  216C'   B5                    	ORA	L
  216D'   CA 220A'              	JZ	SUBBAD		; ':0s' -- there is no line 0
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-104


  2170'   2A 26D3'              	LHLD	SRNG2
  2173'   EB                    	XCHG
  2174'   2A 26D1'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2177'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; CY iff SRNG2 < SRNG1
  217A'   DA 220A'              	JC	SUBBAD		; backwards: vim offers to swap, this refuses
  217D'   EB                    	XCHG			; HL = the last line, DE = the first
  217E'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD
  2181'   23                    	INX	H		; HL = the lines in the range
  2182'   22 26DB'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2185'   2A 26D1'              	LHLD	SRNG1
  2188'   CD 0E40'              	CALL	GOLN
  218B'   C3 2194'              	JMP	SUBLP
  218E'                         SUB_1L:
  218E'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  2191'   22 26DB'              	SHLD	SLINES
  2194'                         SUBLP:
  2194'   CD 2210'              	CALL	SUBLIN		; A = 1 when this line changed
  2197'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2198'   CA 21A6'              	JZ	SUB_NX
  219B'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  219D'   32 26D7'              	STA	SANY
  21A0'   21 0000               	LXI	H,0
  21A3'   22 26D9'              	SHLD	SBACK		; the cursor is ON the last line changed
  21A6'                         SUB_NX:
  21A6'   2A 26DB'              	LHLD	SLINES
  21A9'   2B                    	DCX	H
  21AA'   22 26DB'              	SHLD	SLINES
  21AD'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  21AE'   B5                    	ORA	L
  21AF'   CA 21CD'              	JZ	SUBDON		; the range is spent
  21B2'   21 0001               	LXI	H,1
  21B5'   AF                    	XRA	A
  21B6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; the next line, paging with it
  21B9'   3A 26D7'              	LDA	SANY
  21BC'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21BD'   CA 21C7'              	JZ	SUB_AE		; nothing changed yet: nothing to count from
  21C0'   2A 26D9'              	LHLD	SBACK
  21C3'   23                    	INX	H
  21C4'   22 26D9'              	SHLD	SBACK		; every step taken after the last change has
                                				;   to be walked back at the end -- including
                                				;   this one, when it lands past the last line
  21C7'                         SUB_AE:
  21C7'   CD 0985'              	CALL	ATEOF
  21CA'   D2 2194'              	JNC	SUBLP		; ... unless that ran off the end of the file
  21CD'                         SUBDON:
  21CD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the walk left the count and direction set
  21D0'   3A 26D7'              	LDA	SANY
  21D3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  21D4'   CA 21F2'              	JZ	SUBNFD
  21D7'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  21D9'   32 2622'              	STA	MODF		; the text changed, so ':w' has work to do.  NOT
                                				;   MARKMOD: that also sets DOTCHG, and '.' does
                                				;   not repeat a substitute in vim either (that
                                				;   is '&', which is not built)
  21DC'   2A 26D9'              	LHLD	SBACK
  21DF'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-105


  21E0'   B5                    	ORA	L
  21E1'   CA 21EC'              	JZ	SUB_PL
  21E4'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH
  21E6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLIND		; back to the last line changed
  21E9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  21EC'                         SUB_PL:
  21EC'   CD 0ED7'              	CALL	GPLACE		; the landing: first non-blank, placed as 'G'
  21EF'   C3 06FC'              	JMP	SETFUL		; GPLACE asks for R_MOVE when the target is
                                				;   which rewrote the line under the cursor.
                                				; And that repaint is why a substitute too big
                                				;   for the undo does NOT say so here: EXMSG
                                				;   draws the bottom row there and then, and
                                				;   the full paint this needs takes the status
                                				;   row back (SCRN.MAC says so itself).  'u'
                                				;   carries the message instead -- UNDWHY = 2,
                                				;   at the moment the reader asks for the undo
                                				;   and can still act on the answer.
  21F2'                         SUBNFD:
  21F2'   2A 26DD'              	LHLD	SOFFS
  21F5'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  21F8'   2A 26DF'              	LHLD	SLDLTS
  21FB'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing changed: the row the cursor was on
  21FE'   21 1F9B'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  2201'   CD 1F11'              	CALL	EXERR
  2204'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2207'   C3 1F0C'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
  220A'                         SUBBAD:
  220A'   21 1F89'              	LXI	H,SNOT
  220D'   C3 1F06'              	JMP	EXERT
                                
                                ;SUBLIN -- the substitute on the cursor's line: the first match, or every match
                                ;when 'g' was given.  A = 1 when the line changed.  The cursor is left at the
                                ;line's start either way, so the walk to the next line always starts from one.
                                ;Every offset is re-derived from the cursor after each change, never held
                                ;across one: an insert can page (':%s/0/ZZ/' grows a 100 K file by 12800
                                ;bytes) and paging shifts every logical offset in the window.
  2210'                         SUBLIN:
  2210'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2211'   32 26D8'              	STA	SLCHG
  2214'   CD 228F'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  2217'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET0		; the line has to be RESIDENT before CEND can
  221A'                         SUBL_R:				;   find its end: the pager keeps the CURSOR in
  221A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	RGET		;   the window, not the whole line it sits on,
  221D'   DA 2225'              	JC	SUBL_L		;   and EOLNL only ever scans resident text --
  2220'   FE 0A                 	CPI	LF		;   so a line straddling the resident end came
  2222'   C2 221A'              	JNZ	SUBL_R		;   out short and went unsubstituted (line 179
                                				;   of the 66 K indented file, which starts at
                                				;   byte 2047).  RGET pages through NEEDIN, as
                                				;   every other line reader here does.
  2225'                         SUBL_L:
                                	;--- the scan window: the cursor to the line's content end ---
  2225'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  2228'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  2229'   CD 13CD'              	CALL	CEND
  222C'   D1                    	POP	D
  222D'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = the bytes left on the line
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-106


  2230'   23                    	INX	H		; FWDCNT's own arithmetic, inline: the span it
  2231'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		;   would measure, plus one, less the pattern.
  2234'   7C                    	MOV	A,H		;   (FWDCNT needs a real pointer pair and this
  2235'   B7                    	ORA	A		;   span is a line, not a pair.)
  2236'   FA 2288'              	JM	SUBL_D		; what is left is shorter than the pattern
  2239'   44                    	MOV	B,H
  223A'   4D                    	MOV	C,L		; BC = the start positions a match can take
  223B'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  223E'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; with the gap at the cursor, the line runs
  2241'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF		;   forward from GAPEND
  2244'   DA 2288'              	JC	SUBL_D
  2247'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends past the match; back to its start
  224A'   EB                    	XCHG
  224B'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND
  224E'   EB                    	XCHG
  224F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBHD		; HL = how far into the scan the match is
  2252'   EB                    	XCHG
  2253'   D5                    	PUSH	D		; CUROFF does NOT keep DE (it reads TXTBEG into
  2254'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF		;   it), so the delta goes on the stack
  2257'   D1                    	POP	D
  2258'   19                    	DAD	D		; ... and so the match's logical offset
  2259'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
                                	;--- the first change of the whole substitute re-arms the undo, so 'u'
                                	;    lands on the first match, which is where vim leaves it ---
  225C'   3A 26D7'              	LDA	SANY
  225F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2260'   C2 226D'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  2263'   3A 26D8'              	LDA	SLCHG
  2266'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2267'   C2 226D'              	JNZ	SUBL_C
  226A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	UNDHER		; the cursor snapshot only: UNDBEG would re-run
                                				;   UNDGRW mid-command, and growing the region
                                				;   where a primitive can see it corrupts the
                                				;   gap ('COPY ERR')
  226D'                         SUBL_C:
  226D'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCHBF
  2270'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2271'   26 00                 	MVI	H,0
  2273'   CD 0000*              	CALL	DELFWD		; the match goes
  2276'   11 26E1'              	LXI	D,SREPLN	; ... and the replacement takes its place, the
  2279'   CD 0000*              	CALL	INSSTR		;   way WM splices one: the length byte with
                                				;   its string above it, 'u' capture included
  227C'                         SUBL_I:
  227C'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  227E'   32 26D8'              	STA	SLCHG
  2281'   3A 26D6'              	LDA	SGFLAG
  2284'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2285'   C2 2225'              	JNZ	SUBL_L		; 'g': on from PAST the replacement
  2288'                         SUBL_D:
  2288'   CD 228F'              	CALL	SUBBOL
  228B'   3A 26D8'              	LDA	SLCHG
  228E'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SUBBOL -- the cursor to the start of the line it is on.
  228F'                         SUBBOL:
  228F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-107


  2292'   CD 05C7'              	CALL	BOL
  2295'   C3 0000*              	JMP	GOTO
                                
                                ;VC_SRCH -- '/' and '?': read the pattern, then search.  C is the key, which
                                ; is both the prompt character and the direction.
  2298'                         VC_SRCH:
  2298'   CD 06DB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  229B'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  229C'   CD 066E'              	CALL	GETCNT
  229F'   22 26C9'              	SHLD	SCNT
  22A2'   C1                    	POP	B
  22A3'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  22A4'   FE 2F                 	CPI	'/'
  22A6'   3E 00                 	MVI	A,0
  22A8'   CA 22AD'              	JZ	VS_DIR
  22AB'   3E FF                 	MVI	A,0FFH		; '?' -- backward
  22AD'                         VS_DIR:
  22AD'   32 269F'              	STA	SDIR
  22B0'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST	; the line is read straight into the pattern
  22B3'   3A 0000*              	LDA	SRCMAX		; its capacity is BUF.MAC's to state
  22B6'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  22B7'   CD 1A61'              	CALL	RDLINE
  22BA'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22BB'   C0                    	RNZ			; cancelled: nothing happens at all
  22BC'   3A 2677'              	LDA	RLLEN
  22BF'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22C0'   CA 22D5'              	JZ	VS_OLD		; an empty line repeats the last pattern --
                                				;   RDLINE stored nothing, so it is still there
  22C3'   5F                    	MOV	E,A
  22C4'   16 00                 	MVI	D,0
  22C6'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  22C9'   19                    	DAD	D
  22CA'   36 00                 	MVI	M,0		; 0-terminated, and the length below it:
  22CC'   7B                    	MOV	A,E
  22CD'   32 0000*              	STA	SRCHBF		;   WM's RDSTR layout, built by hand
  22D0'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  22D2'   32 26A1'              	STA	SHAVE
  22D5'                         VS_OLD:
  22D5'   3A 26A1'              	LDA	SHAVE
  22D8'   B7                    	ORA	A
  22D9'   CA 22E5'              	JZ	VS_NOP		; '/' <CR> with nothing ever searched for
  22DC'   3A 269F'              	LDA	SDIR
  22DF'   32 26A0'              	STA	SDIRE
  22E2'   C3 230E'              	JMP	SRCHN
  22E5'                         VS_NOP:
  22E5'   21 1FAF'              	LXI	H,SNOPAT
  22E8'   C3 1F11'              	JMP	EXERR
                                
                                ;VC_N / VCB_N -- 'n' repeats the last search, 'N' reverses its direction.
  22EB'                         VC_N:
  22EB'   CD 06DB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  22EE'   3A 269F'              	LDA	SDIR
  22F1'   C3 22FB'              	JMP	VN_GO
  22F4'                         VCB_N:
  22F4'   CD 06DB'              	CALL	SETMOV
  22F7'   3A 269F'              	LDA	SDIR
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-108


  22FA'   2F                    	CMA			; the other way: 0 <-> FFH
  22FB'                         VN_GO:
  22FB'   32 26A0'              	STA	SDIRE
  22FE'   CD 066E'              	CALL	GETCNT
  2301'   22 26C9'              	SHLD	SCNT
  2304'   3A 26A1'              	LDA	SHAVE
  2307'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2308'   CA 22E5'              	JZ	VS_NOP
  230B'   C3 230E'              	JMP	SRCHN
                                
                                ;SRCHN -- the count, one whole-file search each.  Not found at ANY repeat
                                ; leaves the cursor where the command started, as vim does, and says so; the
                                ; message wants the screen left alone, which is what EXERR's R_MOVE is for.
  230E'                         SRCHN:
  230E'   CD 0EBF'              	CALL	GZONE		; the screen's top line, before the cursor moves
  2311'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	LNDLT
  2314'   22 26CD'              	SHLD	SLDLT		; the sweep moves the cursor with PUTCUR, which
                                				;   does not maintain LNDLT (GOTO does)
  2317'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CUROFF
  231A'   22 26CB'              	SHLD	SOFF
  231D'                         SN_LP:
  231D'   CD 2355'              	CALL	SRCHGO
  2320'   DA 233A'              	JC	SN_BAD
  2323'   2A 26C9'              	LHLD	SCNT
  2326'   2B                    	DCX	H
  2327'   22 26C9'              	SHLD	SCNT
  232A'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  232B'   B5                    	ORA	L
  232C'   C2 231D'              	JNZ	SN_LP
  232F'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT		; the sweep left CMDCNT wide open (and DIRFLG
                                				;   backward): WM clears both per command
  2332'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2334'   32 26AC'              	STA	GPKEEP		; land on the match, not the first non-blank
  2337'   C3 0ED7'              	JMP	GPLACE		; (which sets LNDLT itself)
  233A'                         SN_BAD:
  233A'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CLRCNT
  233D'   2A 26CB'              	LHLD	SOFF
  2340'   CD 0000*              	CALL	GOTO
  2343'   2A 26CD'              	LHLD	SLDLT
  2346'   22 0000*              	SHLD	LNDLT		; nothing moved: the row the cursor was on
  2349'   21 1F9B'              	LXI	H,SNOTFD
  234C'   CD 1F11'              	CALL	EXERR
  234F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,SRCHST
  2352'   C3 1F0C'              	JMP	EXOUT		; 'Pattern not found: ' and the pattern
                                
                                ;SRCHGO -- one search of the whole file, wrapping once: from the cursor to the
                                ; end in SDIRE's direction, then from the other end back to it.  CY = the
                                ; pattern is nowhere in the file (and the cursor is left at an end of it --
                                ; SRCHN is what puts it back).
  2355'                         SRCHGO:
  2355'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1
  2357'   32 26CF'              	STA	SSKIP		; this pass starts one past the cursor
  235A'   CD 236C'              	CALL	SRCHW
  235D'   D0                    	RNC
  235E'   3A 26A0'              	LDA	SDIRE
  2361'   2F                    	CMA
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-109


  2362'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MVLMAX		; to the end the wrapped pass starts from
  2365'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2366'   32 26CF'              	STA	SSKIP		; ... but the wrapped pass starts AT it, or a
                                				;   match on the file's very first character
                                				;   would be stepped over and never found
  2369'   C3 236C'              	JMP	SRCHW
                                
                                ;SRCHW -- WM FINDA (WM 1943): the file from the cursor on, one resident
                                ; window at a time.  A miss moves the cursor to that window's edge and pages
                                ; one step (SCRLST), so the search runs off the window and through the file;
                                ; CY when the file runs out.  PAGESTP only pages while the count is unspent,
                                ; so the sweep opens CMDCNT wide, as MVLMAX does.  WM's FINDA then puts the
                                ; cursor exactly at the file's end (WM 1949); nothing here needs that -- the
                                ; caller either wraps to the other end or restores the cursor outright.
  236C'                         SRCHW:
  236C'   3A 26A0'              	LDA	SDIRE
  236F'   32 0000*              	STA	DIRFLG
  2372'   21 FFFF               	LXI	H,0FFFFH
  2375'   22 261F'              	SHLD	CMDCNT
  2378'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2379'   32 0000*              	STA	AUXCNT
  237C'                         SW_LP:
  237C'   CD 2388'              	CALL	SRCH1
  237F'   D0                    	RNC
  2380'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SCRLST
  2383'   D2 237C'              	JNC	SW_LP
  2386'   37                    	STC
  2387'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;SRCH1 -- WM FINDB (WM 1984) without its RDSTR: one search inside the
                                ; resident window, DIRFLG picking the direction.  CY = not in this window; on
                                ; a hit the cursor is ON the match's first character.
  2388'                         SRCH1:
  2388'   11 0000*              	LXI	D,SRCHST
  238B'   3A 0000*              	LDA	DIRFLG
  238E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  238F'   FA 23B3'              	JM	SR1_BK
  2392'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,GAPEND
  2395'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  2398'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPEND		; WM loads the pointer BEFORE testing the
  239B'   D8                    	RC			;   carry: a miss still has to leave HL a
                                				;   real text address, because SCRLST puts
                                				;   the cursor there on the way to paging
  239C'   3A 26CF'              	LDA	SSKIP
  239F'   B7                    	ORA	A
  23A0'   CA 23A9'              	JZ	SR1_FW
  23A3'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  23A4'   B1                    	ORA	C
  23A5'   37                    	STC
  23A6'   C8                    	RZ			; no room for a match even before the skip
  23A7'   0B                    	DCX	B		; vim's '/' starts one character PAST the
  23A8'   23                    	INX	H		;   cursor, so the character under it can
                                				;   never be the one that matches
  23A9'                         SR1_FW:
  23A9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHF
  23AC'   D8                    	RC
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-110


  23AD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHF ends PAST the match; vi lands on it
  23B0'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
  23B3'                         SR1_BK:
  23B3'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TXTBEG
  23B6'   CD 0000*              	CALL	FWDCNT
  23B9'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	GAPBEG		; (as above: the pointer, then the carry)
  23BC'   D8                    	RC
  23BD'   CD 0000*              	CALL	SUBLEN		; MATCHB already lands on the first character
  23C0'   CD 0000*              	CALL	MATCHB
  23C3'   D8                    	RC
  23C4'   C3 0000*              	JMP	PUTCUR
                                
                                ;============================================================
                                ; DISPATCH TABLES -- the messages first, then CMDTAB and ACTTAB.
                                ;============================================================
                                ;============================================================
                                ; MESSAGES -- the bottom row is vim's message line: blank unless something has
                                ; been said on it.  A message is built by successive calls -- EXPRM first, which
                                ; clears the row, then the pieces straight to the terminal.  There is no
                                ; concatenation buffer and none is wanted: the pieces are a name, a flag and a
                                ; number, and each knows how to write itself.
                                ;
                                ; Every message is drawn AFTER the frame (MSGPST, from the main loop), because
                                ; a full repaint blanks the bottom row -- so a message written during the
                                ; command would be wiped by the repaint that same key causes.
                                ;============================================================
                                
                                ;MSGNAM -- open the row and write the file name at HL (a 12-byte FCB field:
                                ; drive, 8 name, 3 type) in quotes, the way vim opens a message: "TEST.TXT".
                                ; A blank name field is vim's "[No Name]".  vim writes a path relative to the
                                ; current directory -- a file in it bare, "../x/VI.MAC" otherwise -- and the
                                ; logged drive is CP/M's current directory, so the drive is written only when
                                ; it is another one: "B:TEST.TXT".
  23C7'                         MSGNAM:
  23C7'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  23C8'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  23CA'   CD 0000*              	CALL	EXPRM		; clears the row, writes the opening quote
  23CD'   E1                    	POP	H
  23CE'   E5                    	PUSH	H		; keep the base: the type field is 9 past it
  23CF'   23                    	INX	H
  23D0'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  23D1'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  23D3'   C2 23E0'              	JNZ	MN_HAS
  23D6'   21 200B'              	LXI	H,SNONM
  23D9'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  23DC'   E1                    	POP	H
  23DD'   C3 242A'              	JMP	MN_END
  23E0'                         MN_HAS:
  23E0'   0E 19                 	MVI	C,BF_CURDSK
  23E2'   CD 0005               	CALL	BDOS		; A = the logged drive, A: = 0
  23E5'   E1                    	POP	H
  23E6'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  23E7'   3C                    	INR	A		; the FCB's drive byte is explicit, A: = 1
  23E8'   BE                    	CMP	M
  23E9'   CA 23F7'              	JZ	MN_NAM
  23EC'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-111


  23ED'   C6 40                 	ADI	'A'-1
  23EF'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  23F2'   3E 3A                 	MVI	A,':'
  23F4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  23F7'                         MN_NAM:
  23F7'   E1                    	POP	H
  23F8'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  23F9'   23                    	INX	H
  23FA'   0E 08                 	MVI	C,8
  23FC'                         MN_NM:
  23FC'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  23FD'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  23FF'   CA 240A'              	JZ	MN_EXT
  2402'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2405'   23                    	INX	H
  2406'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2407'   C2 23FC'              	JNZ	MN_NM
  240A'                         MN_EXT:
  240A'   E1                    	POP	H
  240B'   11 0009               	LXI	D,9
  240E'   19                    	DAD	D		; the type field
  240F'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  2410'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2412'   CA 242A'              	JZ	MN_END
  2415'   3E 2E                 	MVI	A,'.'
  2417'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  241A'   0E 03                 	MVI	C,3
  241C'                         MN_XL:
  241C'   7E                    	MOV	A,M
  241D'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  241F'   CA 242A'              	JZ	MN_END
  2422'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2425'   23                    	INX	H
  2426'   0D                    	DCR	C
  2427'   C2 241C'              	JNZ	MN_XL
  242A'                         MN_END:
  242A'   3E 22                 	MVI	A,'"'
  242C'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTCH
                                
                                ;MSGFLG -- the flags after the name, in vim's order and with vim's spelling:
                                ; [Modified][readonly][New], run together with ONE space before the first of
                                ; them (measured from vim 9.1).  A = 0 leaves [Modified] out, which is the load
                                ; message, where nothing has been changed yet.
  242F'                         MSGFLG:
  242F'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0		; B: a flag has been written (the space goes once)
  2431'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2432'   CA 2442'              	JZ	MF_RO
  2435'   3A 2622'              	LDA	MODF
  2438'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2439'   CA 2442'              	JZ	MF_RO
  243C'   21 2015'              	LXI	H,SMODM
  243F'   CD 2457'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  2442'                         MF_RO:
  2442'   3A 268D'              	LDA	ROFLAG
  2445'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2446'   CA 244F'              	JZ	MF_NEW
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-112


  2449'   21 2020'              	LXI	H,SROM
  244C'   CD 2457'              	CALL	MF_PUT
  244F'                         MF_NEW:
  244F'   3A 268B'              	LDA	MSGNEW
  2452'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2453'   C8                    	RZ
  2454'   21 202B'              	LXI	H,SNEWM
  2457'                         MF_PUT:
  2457'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2458'   B7                    	ORA	A
  2459'   C2 2465'              	JNZ	MF_STR
  245C'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  245E'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  245F'   3E 20                 	MVI	A,' '
  2461'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  2464'   E1                    	POP	H
  2465'                         MF_STR:
  2465'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;EMITW -- HL as unsigned decimal, no leading zeros (0 still prints one digit).
                                ; Repeated subtraction of each power of ten, which is all an 8080 has.  B
                                ; carries 'a digit has been printed' so the leading zeros are skipped.
  2468'                         EMITW:
  2468'   C5                    	PUSH	B
  2469'   D5                    	PUSH	D
  246A'   06 00                 	MVI	B,0
  246C'   11 2710               	LXI	D,10000
  246F'   CD 248D'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2472'   11 03E8               	LXI	D,1000
  2475'   CD 248D'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2478'   11 0064               	LXI	D,100
  247B'   CD 248D'              	CALL	EWDIG
  247E'   11 000A               	LXI	D,10
  2481'   CD 248D'              	CALL	EWDIG
  2484'   7D                    	MOV	A,L		; the units digit is always printed
  2485'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  2487'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  248A'   D1                    	POP	D
  248B'   C1                    	POP	B
  248C'   C9                    	RET
  248D'                         EWDIG:
  248D'   0E 00                 	MVI	C,0
  248F'                         EW_LP:
  248F'   7D                    	MOV	A,L
  2490'   93                    	SUB	E
  2491'   6F                    	MOV	L,A
  2492'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  2493'   9A                    	SBB	D
  2494'   67                    	MOV	H,A
  2495'   DA 249C'              	JC	EW_DN
  2498'   0C                    	INR	C
  2499'   C3 248F'              	JMP	EW_LP
  249C'                         EW_DN:
  249C'   19                    	DAD	D		; one subtraction too many: give it back
  249D'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  249E'   B7                    	ORA	A
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-113


  249F'   C2 24A5'              	JNZ	EW_PR
  24A2'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  24A3'   B7                    	ORA	A
  24A4'   C8                    	RZ			; a leading zero: write nothing
  24A5'                         EW_PR:
  24A5'   79                    	MOV	A,C
  24A6'   C6 30                 	ADI	'0'
  24A8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
  24AB'   06 01                 	MVI	B,1
  24AD'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;ISEMPT -- Z iff the buffer holds nothing at all: no resident text, and nothing
                                ; spilled behind the window either.  vim's '--No lines in buffer--', shortened.
  24AE'                         ISEMPT:
  24AE'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	OUTPOS
  24B1'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  24B2'   B5                    	ORA	L
  24B3'   C0                    	RNZ
  24B4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	LOGLEN
  24B7'   7C                    	MOV	A,H
  24B8'   B5                    	ORA	L
  24B9'   C9                    	RET
                                
                                ;MSGCTG -- '^G': the file, its flags, and where the cursor is.  DEVIATION: vim
                                ; also gives the file's line total and a percentage of it; no line total is kept
                                ; anywhere here, and counting one means a sweep of the whole file out to EOF and
                                ; a page back -- which would cost the undo region (PGTOPM) as well as the wait.
  24BA'                         MSGCTG:
  24BA'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  24BD'   CD 23C7'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  24C0'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,1		; '^G' is where vim shows [Modified]
  24C2'   CD 242F'              	CALL	MSGFLG
  24C5'   CD 24AE'              	CALL	ISEMPT
  24C8'   C2 24D1'              	JNZ	MG_POS
  24CB'   21 203E'              	LXI	H,SNOLIN
  24CE'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
  24D1'                         MG_POS:
  24D1'   21 2031'              	LXI	H,SLINE
  24D4'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  24D7'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CNTLF		; the line feeds before the cursor ...
  24DA'   23                    	INX	H		;   ... so the line it is on
  24DB'   CD 2468'              	CALL	EMITW
  24DE'   21 2038'              	LXI	H,SCOLM
  24E1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTSTR
  24E4'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CBOL		; the byte column within the line, 0-based
  24E7'   23                    	INX	H
  24E8'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24E9'   CD 2468'              	CALL	EMITW
  24EC'   2A 0000*              	LHLD	CURDCL		; the column a TAB expands that byte to
  24EF'   23                    	INX	H
  24F0'   D1                    	POP	D
  24F1'   CD 0000*              	CALL	CMP16		; the same -> vim writes one number, not two
  24F4'   C8                    	RZ
  24F5'   E5                    	PUSH	H
  24F6'   3E 2D                 	MVI	A,'-'
  24F8'   CD 0000*              	CALL	OUTCH
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-114


  24FB'   E1                    	POP	H
  24FC'   C3 2468'              	JMP	EMITW
                                
                                ;FILMSG -- the load message: the file and its flags.  vim adds the line and
                                ; byte totals ('3L, 30B'); the line total is the sweep MSGCTG's note describes.
                                ; A buffer with no file name gets no message at all, as in vim.
  24FF'                         FILMSG:
  24FF'   3A 0001*              	LDA	FNAME+1
  2502'   FE 20                 	CPI	' '
  2504'   C8                    	RZ
  2505'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,FNAME
  2508'   CD 23C7'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  250B'   AF                    	XRA	A		; nothing has been changed yet
  250C'   C3 242F'              	JMP	MSGFLG
                                
                                ;WRMSG -- what ':w' says.  vim counts what it wrote ('"TEST.TXT" 3L, 29B
                                ; written'); the counts are the same sweep, so this says only that it happened.
                                ; The name is TFCB's, so ':w OTHER.TXT' names the file it really wrote.
  250F'                         WRMSG:
  250F'   21 0000*              	LXI	H,TFCB
  2512'   CD 23C7'              	CALL	MSGNAM
  2515'   21 2050'              	LXI	H,SWRIT
  2518'   C3 0000*              	JMP	OUTSTR
                                
                                ;MSGPST -- draw the message this key asked for, once the frame is painted, and
                                ; put the cursor back.  The main loop calls it after SCDRAW and MODEMS.
  251B'                         MSGPST:
  251B'   3A 268A'              	LDA	MSGWHT
  251E'   B7                    	ORA	A
  251F'   C8                    	RZ
  2520'   47                    	MOV	B,A
  2521'   AF                    	XRA	A
  2522'   32 268A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2525'   78                    	MOV	A,B
  2526'   FE 01                 	CPI	MW_FIL
  2528'   CA 2536'              	JZ	MP_FIL
  252B'   FE 02                 	CPI	MW_WRT
  252D'   CA 253C'              	JZ	MP_WRT
  2530'   CD 24BA'              	CALL	MSGCTG
  2533'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  2536'                         MP_FIL:
  2536'   CD 24FF'              	CALL	FILMSG
  2539'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
  253C'                         MP_WRT:
  253C'   CD 250F'              	CALL	WRMSG
  253F'   C3 0000*              	JMP	SCURS
                                
                                ;VC_CTLG -- '^G'.  It changes nothing and moves nothing, so the frame is a
                                ; still one and the message goes on after it.
  2542'                         VC_CTLG:
  2542'   3E 03                 	MVI	A,MW_CTG
  2544'   32 268A'              	STA	MSGWHT
  2547'   3E 01                 	MVI	A,R_MOVE
  2549'   32 2623'              	STA	RINTENT
  254C'   C9                    	RET
                                
	MACRO-80 3.44	09-Dec-81	PAGE	1-115


  254D'                         CMDTAB:
